WEBVTT

00:00:00.138 --> 00:00:01.975
Here's something that
happens to almost everyone:

00:00:02.395 --> 00:00:04.695
It's election day. You
get to your polling booth,

00:00:04.755 --> 00:00:08.255
work your way down your ballot, and
somewhere past the top of it the

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:09.775
names stop meaning anything.

00:00:10.473 --> 00:00:12.859
You're not uninformed.
You are out of time.

00:00:13.439 --> 00:00:16.419
There were forty races on that
ballot and you had a lunch break.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.943
So you do what the ballot makes easy.

00:00:18.943 --> 00:00:21.523
You look for the letter next to the name,

00:00:21.523 --> 00:00:23.563
and you let the letter decide.

00:00:24.302 --> 00:00:27.850
That letter is doing an enormous
amount of work for one character.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:31.210
And it's worth asking, how much
does the letter actually earn?

00:00:31.773 --> 00:00:34.033
This is Empowered Compass.

00:00:35.762 --> 00:00:37.597
A party label is a bundle of stances.

00:00:38.097 --> 00:00:41.697
Dozens of positions, packed
together by somebody who is not you,

00:00:41.697 --> 00:00:44.457
and handed to you as a single choice.

00:00:45.169 --> 00:00:49.831
Put two people in the same box and
they will still disagree about plenty.

00:00:50.591 --> 00:00:52.511
The box doesn't promise otherwise.

00:00:53.393 --> 00:00:55.393
And if you have two
people in different boxes,

00:00:56.093 --> 00:00:59.133
they tend to agree about far
more than either of them expects.

00:01:00.013 --> 00:01:02.353
The boxes just make that hard to see.

00:01:02.999 --> 00:01:06.131
So the letter answers a question;
just not the one you were asking.

00:01:06.491 --> 00:01:10.011
It tells you whose team
someone is on, not whether they

00:01:10.011 --> 00:01:11.591
would vote the way you would.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.273
The Empowered Compass
starts from the other end.

00:01:14.753 --> 00:01:16.473
Not with the parties and their bundles,

00:01:17.193 --> 00:01:21.537
but with you, and the handful of
things you actually care about.

00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:24.321
Voter driven, not party driven.

00:01:25.081 --> 00:01:28.461
That's the whole idea,
putting what citizens want at

00:01:28.461 --> 00:01:29.921
the heart of our democracy.

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:33.175
We call these things
your vote-worthy issues.

00:01:33.855 --> 00:01:35.255
Not every opinion you hold.

00:01:35.575 --> 00:01:38.675
Just the few that would genuinely
change who you vote for.

00:01:39.263 --> 00:01:42.379
The goal is to turn your
priorities into a picture.

00:01:42.999 --> 00:01:45.619
One you can read in a
second and hold up against

00:01:45.619 --> 00:01:47.859
anybody asking for your vote.

00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:50.112
You start by calibrating your compass.

00:01:50.452 --> 00:01:52.092
Which is done through
a quick questionnaire.

00:01:52.818 --> 00:01:54.103
It asks two different things.

00:01:54.423 --> 00:01:57.163
First, what issues belong
on your list at all?

00:01:57.823 --> 00:02:01.486
And second, for each topic,
where do you actually land?

00:02:02.106 --> 00:02:03.966
What does your solution look like?

00:02:04.569 --> 00:02:08.444
You choose which issues. We show you
how you how candidates feel on those

00:02:08.444 --> 00:02:12.264
issues. And then you choose
how you want to vote.

00:02:12.862 --> 00:02:15.497
What comes out the other side
is not a score and not a label.

00:02:16.137 --> 00:02:19.317
It is a customized shape
that represents you.

00:02:19.987 --> 00:02:21.968
Take one issue on its own. Like Housing.

00:02:22.657 --> 00:02:24.570
If that seems like a
vote-worthy issue to you,

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.570
then it becomes a spoke. A line
running out from a centre point,

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:32.990
with the realistic answers to it
set out in order along the line.

00:02:33.557 --> 00:02:35.388
We sort common answers along a spectrum.

00:02:35.888 --> 00:02:38.968
For instance with housing, at one
end we have the government not

00:02:38.968 --> 00:02:41.048
intervening and letting
the markets decide,

00:02:41.548 --> 00:02:44.808
and at the other end we have the
government building low income housing.

00:02:45.474 --> 00:02:47.574
You choose the answer that fits you best,

00:02:48.094 --> 00:02:51.674
or you write your own. Your
dot sits on that answer.

00:02:52.254 --> 00:02:54.594
The spoke is not measuring
how strongly you feel.

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:57.334
It is recording which solution you want.

00:02:57.989 --> 00:02:59.952
Then do that for each issue on your list.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.530
Each topic gets a spoke,
and each spoke gets your

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.230
dot describing your stance.

00:03:06.063 --> 00:03:10.223
Then join them up. The outline that
connects your points is your compass.

00:03:11.001 --> 00:03:13.521
And a shape is a thing a
person can actually read.

00:03:13.861 --> 00:03:16.361
Not a number, not a
percentage, not a side.

00:03:17.081 --> 00:03:19.401
A shape you recognise as yours.

00:03:20.339 --> 00:03:23.260
Once it's calibrated you can
lay something else over it.

00:03:23.738 --> 00:03:28.058
Bring in a civic leader. Their
positions get placed on the same spokes,

00:03:28.058 --> 00:03:31.718
at the same scale, and
drawn as a second shape.

00:03:32.333 --> 00:03:34.193
Where the two shapes
cover the same ground,

00:03:34.553 --> 00:03:39.253
you agree. That region is real
agreement, on the issues you picked.

00:03:39.861 --> 00:03:43.435
And where your shapes don't align,
you are looking at something specific.

00:03:44.189 --> 00:03:47.517
The issues you never put on your
list stay off the picture entirely.

00:03:48.255 --> 00:03:49.755
And notice what the tool does not do.

00:03:50.295 --> 00:03:52.755
It does not rank them
for you, or pick for you,

00:03:52.955 --> 00:03:55.135
or tell you who your candidate is.

00:03:55.895 --> 00:03:59.752
It draws both shapes
honestly and lets you look.

00:04:00.314 --> 00:04:02.088
Which leaves the question
that matters most.

00:04:02.938 --> 00:04:04.748
Where did their dot come from?

00:04:05.128 --> 00:04:08.128
Who decided that this is
where this person stands?

00:04:08.885 --> 00:04:12.845
Nobody guessed. Every stance we
place on a spoke is researched,

00:04:13.145 --> 00:04:15.445
one person and one topic at a time.

00:04:16.345 --> 00:04:18.925
We pick the answer on the ladder
that their record actually

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:21.305
supports, and we write down why.

00:04:21.977 --> 00:04:25.397
And we keep the receipts. Each
one carries its references.

00:04:26.137 --> 00:04:28.077
Voting records. Bill texts.

00:04:28.997 --> 00:04:32.317
Legislature sites. The
public record it came out of.

00:04:32.817 --> 00:04:34.337
You can open any of them yourself.

00:04:35.055 --> 00:04:36.951
That is most of the work behind this tool.

00:04:37.791 --> 00:04:41.051
Tens of thousands of positions,
across thousands of officials,

00:04:41.051 --> 00:04:44.011
with tens of thousands
of references under them.

00:04:44.564 --> 00:04:45.682
And this one is not a sketch.

00:04:46.562 --> 00:04:51.433
The Empowered Compass is live today,
at compass dot empowered dot vote.

00:04:53.106 --> 00:04:56.006
Calibrate once. After
that, every election,

00:04:56.386 --> 00:04:58.846
you hold your shape up
against whoever is asking,

00:04:59.146 --> 00:05:00.606
and you decide for yourself.

00:05:01.401 --> 00:05:04.995
That's the Empowered Compass.
Now go draw your own.
